  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "Registration order is load-bearing",
      "body": "index.js:77 lists every module; listeners and tools run in that order, so usage sits last to meter every request that an earlier listener rewrote. A new module must be placed, not appended.",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "ds-bot/index.js"
    },
    {
      "title": "The team lives in one JSON file",
      "body": "store.js keeps bots, rooms, agents, capabilities, exchanges and prefs in state.json, writes it atomically, and takes state.lock so a second host cannot write. Everything else indexes into it.",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "ds-bot/src/host/store.js"
    },
    {
      "title": "Three long modules hold most of the logic",
      "body": "memory.js 1,748, parts.js 1,325 and groups.js 1,000 are 4.1k of the host's 16k lines. Each mixes state, tools and I/O, so a change crosses file boundaries by convention only.",
      "severity": "warn",
      "path": "ds-bot/src/host/memory.js"
    },
    {
      "title": "DSH is a dependency, never patched",
      "body": "Capabilities go through ctx.tools, ctx.sessions, ctx.llm or ctx.webServer; AGENTS.md rule 1 forbids patching DSH, and docs/plans/2026-10-13-dsh-concessions.md records the compromises that forced.",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "ds-bot/index.js"
    },
    {
      "title": "The client is reached two ways",
      "body": "channel.js answers POST /api/bot and feed.js pushes SSE; browser.js adds a third long-poll path, so the host has three client-facing protocols, each with its own timeout and staleness rules.",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "ds-bot/src/host/feed.js"
    },
    {
      "title": "Approvals are answered, not surfaced",
      "body": "A member's group Session is hidden, so DSH's approval card cannot show; approvals.js answers those requests itself, which is why a group turn cannot block on a prompt no one sees.",
      "severity": "info",
      "path": "ds-bot/src/host/approvals.js"
    }
  ]
}